1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:05,120
Imagine putting away R3,833 a month.

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:09,280
In about eleven years you stop…
and your money just keeps growing,

3
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:11,440
for the rest of your working life.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,040
And SARS never takes a
single cent of that growth.

5
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,360
That's not a trick. It's the law.

6
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,920
It's called a Tax-Free Savings Account,

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,400
and many South Africans either don't
have one… or aren't using it properly.

8
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:27,280
In this video, you'll
learn exactly how it works,

9
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:32,320
the two mistakes that cost people money,
and how to open one, step by step.

10
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,000
Let's start from zero.

11
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,360
First, what is a Tax-Free Savings Account?

12
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:39,680
It's not a special type of investment.

13
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,280
Think of it as a container.

14
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:44,960
A wrapper. Inside the wrapper,
you can hold different things:

15
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:48,240
a simple savings account
at your bank, a unit trust,

16
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:51,147
or an ETF that follows the stock market.

17
00:00:51,173 --> 00:00:52,987
The wrapper's job is simple.

18
00:00:53,093 --> 00:00:56,720
Whatever your money earns
inside it is protected from tax.

19
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,920
TFSAs started on 1 March 2015,

20
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:04,480
and today most banks and investment
platforms in South Africa offer one.

21
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:06,880
So which taxes do you avoid?

22
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,080
Normally, your money
gets taxed in three ways.

23
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,893
Interest is taxed as income, once
it's above your yearly exemption.

24
00:01:13,947 --> 00:01:18,400
That's R23,800 a year if you're under 65.

25
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,640
Dividends from shares are taxed at 20%.

26
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,200
And when an investment
grows and you sell it,

27
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:29,200
you can pay capital gains tax on
the profit above R50,000 a year.

28
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:32,280
Inside a TFSA, none of that applies.

29
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:34,000
No income tax on interest.

30
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:37,200
No dividends tax. No capital gains tax.

31
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:38,520
And here's a bonus:

32
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:44,453
TFSA interest doesn't even use up your
normal R23,800 interest exemption.

33
00:01:44,507 --> 00:01:46,600
You keep that for your other savings.

34
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,080
Let's make that real.

35
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:53,080
Say you have R500,000 in a normal
savings account earning 8% a year.

36
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:55,520
That's R40,000 of interest.

37
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,680
The first R23,800 is exempt,

38
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:02,040
so R16,200 is taxable.

39
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:07,160
If you pay 26% tax,
that's about R4,200 to SARS.

40
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,440
Every single year. Inside
a TFSA, that bill is zero.

41
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:13,960
Of course, there's a limit.

42
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,400
Actually, there are two.

43
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:17,520
The yearly limit. From 1 March 2026,

44
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:22,440
you can put in up to R46,000 per tax year.

45
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,000
That's about R3,833 a month.

46
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:29,520
When TFSAs started, the limit was R30,000.

47
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:34,320
It went up to R33,000, then R36,000,

48
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:38,800
and now R46,000,
the biggest increase so far.

49
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:44,240
And the lifetime limit: R500,000
per person, for your whole life.

50
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:46,440
One thing catches people out.

51
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,520
Unused space does not carry over.

52
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:52,960
If you only put in R20,000 this tax year,

53
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:56,080
the other R26,000 is gone forever.

54
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,560
Next year, you start again at R46,000.

55
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,640
At the full amount,

56
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:06,400
it takes about eleven years to
reach the R500,000 lifetime limit.

57
00:03:07,287 --> 00:03:13,047
Now the best part. The limits only
count what you put in… not what it grows

58
00:03:13,127 --> 00:03:15,287
into. Let's look at an example.

59
00:03:15,767 --> 00:03:18,967
These numbers are only an
illustration, not a promise.

60
00:03:19,527 --> 00:03:25,207
Say you start at 25.
You put in R3,833 a month

61
00:03:25,527 --> 00:03:30,247
until you reach the R500,000
limit, at about age 36.

62
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:34,127
Then you stop paying in completely,
and you just leave it.

63
00:03:34,487 --> 00:03:38,887
If it grows at 5% a year
above inflation, after fees,

64
00:03:38,967 --> 00:03:44,087
then by 65 you'd have about
R2.7 million, in today's money.

65
00:03:44,727 --> 00:03:47,687
Only R500,000 of that came from you.

66
00:03:48,167 --> 00:03:53,567
About 82% is growth… and
every rand of it is tax-free.

67
00:03:54,007 --> 00:03:56,727
Start ten years later, at 35,

68
00:03:56,767 --> 00:04:00,367
and you'd end up with
about R1.7 million instead.

69
00:04:00,647 --> 00:04:04,167
Same contributions.
Ten fewer years of growth.

70
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:07,287
That's why starting early matters so much.

71
00:04:07,591 --> 00:04:09,127
But there are two traps.

72
00:04:09,367 --> 00:04:12,087
Trap number one: going over the limit.

73
00:04:12,327 --> 00:04:15,767
The limits are for you as
a person, not per account.

74
00:04:16,247 --> 00:04:18,887
You're allowed to have more than one TFSA,

75
00:04:19,007 --> 00:04:22,327
but everything you put into
all of them counts together.

76
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:26,567
And your bank can only see its
own account, so it can't warn you.

77
00:04:26,767 --> 00:04:28,967
Keeping track is your job.

78
00:04:29,047 --> 00:04:33,687
If you go over, SARS charges
a 40% penalty on the extra.

79
00:04:33,767 --> 00:04:38,967
Here's SARS's own example:
you put in R50,000 in one tax year.

80
00:04:39,007 --> 00:04:43,607
The limit is R46,000,
so you're R4,000 over.

81
00:04:43,687 --> 00:04:48,647
Forty percent of R4,000
is a R1,600 penalty.

82
00:04:48,967 --> 00:04:51,127
Even a tiny mistake counts.

83
00:04:51,367 --> 00:04:55,927
A debit order of R3,834 a month adds up to

84
00:04:56,047 --> 00:04:58,247
R46,008 for the year.

85
00:04:58,647 --> 00:05:00,767
That's R8 over the limit.

86
00:05:01,047 --> 00:05:03,767
Trap number two: taking money out.

87
00:05:04,007 --> 00:05:07,087
You can withdraw from a TFSA at any time.

88
00:05:07,287 --> 00:05:09,647
But the space you used is gone.

89
00:05:09,927 --> 00:05:13,287
If you take out R10,000
and later put it back,

90
00:05:13,367 --> 00:05:16,807
SARS treats that as a
new R10,000 contribution.

91
00:05:17,207 --> 00:05:21,447
It counts against your yearly limit
and your lifetime limit again.

92
00:05:21,767 --> 00:05:24,727
So a TFSA is not your emergency fund.

93
00:05:25,207 --> 00:05:28,167
Keep emergency money in
a normal savings account,

94
00:05:28,247 --> 00:05:31,207
and let your TFSA do the long, slow work.

95
00:05:31,687 --> 00:05:33,447
What if you find a better provider?

96
00:05:33,847 --> 00:05:35,127
You don't have to withdraw.

97
00:05:35,367 --> 00:05:40,007
You can transfer. Ask your new
provider to move your TFSA across.

98
00:05:40,487 --> 00:05:44,887
A proper transfer is not a new contribution,
so it doesn't touch your limits.

99
00:05:45,447 --> 00:05:49,287
The rules say it must be done within
ten business days of your request.

100
00:05:49,847 --> 00:05:54,887
What you must never do is withdraw the money
yourself and deposit it somewhere else.

101
00:05:54,967 --> 00:05:57,927
That counts as a brand new contribution.

102
00:05:58,815 --> 00:06:01,775
So what should you put inside your TFSA?

103
00:06:01,855 --> 00:06:03,495
There are two main options.

104
00:06:03,695 --> 00:06:07,535
A cash option, like a bank
savings or fixed deposit account.

105
00:06:08,015 --> 00:06:11,935
It's simple and safe,
but the growth is usually slow,

106
00:06:12,015 --> 00:06:15,375
and fixed terms can lock your
money for up to five years.

107
00:06:15,775 --> 00:06:20,095
Or an investment option,
like a unit trust or an ETF.

108
00:06:20,415 --> 00:06:23,775
An ETF can follow a
whole stock market index,

109
00:06:23,855 --> 00:06:28,015
so one purchase spreads your money
across many companies at once.

110
00:06:28,335 --> 00:06:31,535
The value goes up and
down, sometimes a lot,

111
00:06:31,655 --> 00:06:35,295
but over long periods it
has more room to grow.

112
00:06:35,375 --> 00:06:39,935
That's why many people use their TFSA for
money they won't need for ten years or

113
00:06:40,015 --> 00:06:42,815
more. One thing you can't do:

114
00:06:42,975 --> 00:06:47,375
buy individual company shares
directly inside a TFSA.

115
00:06:47,415 --> 00:06:49,855
You invest through funds instead.

116
00:06:49,935 --> 00:06:52,015
The rules also protect you.

117
00:06:52,295 --> 00:06:57,535
Providers can't charge performance fees
on a TFSA, fees must be reasonable,

118
00:06:57,575 --> 00:07:01,535
and withdrawals must be paid
out within seven business days.

119
00:07:01,615 --> 00:07:06,495
But fees still differ between products,
so always compare before you choose.

120
00:07:06,815 --> 00:07:11,615
A common question: should you use
a TFSA or a retirement annuity?

121
00:07:12,175 --> 00:07:14,175
They work in opposite ways.

122
00:07:14,495 --> 00:07:18,975
A retirement annuity gives you a
tax deduction now, when you pay in,

123
00:07:19,095 --> 00:07:21,135
but you're taxed when you take it out,

124
00:07:21,215 --> 00:07:24,015
and most of your money stays
locked until retirement.

125
00:07:24,655 --> 00:07:29,935
A TFSA gives you no deduction now,
but everything comes out tax-free,

126
00:07:30,055 --> 00:07:32,735
and you can reach your
money if you really need to.

127
00:07:33,055 --> 00:07:38,415
They're not rivals. Many people use both:
the retirement annuity for retirement,

128
00:07:38,495 --> 00:07:42,015
and the TFSA for long-term
goals and extra freedom.

129
00:07:42,575 --> 00:07:45,415
You can also open a
TFSA for your children.

130
00:07:45,695 --> 00:07:49,855
A child's TFSA uses the
child's own limits, not yours.

131
00:07:50,255 --> 00:07:54,815
So you're using up part of
their R500,000 lifetime limit,

132
00:07:54,895 --> 00:07:56,895
which can give them a huge head start.

133
00:07:57,455 --> 00:07:59,775
Just remember: when money is withdrawn,

134
00:07:59,855 --> 00:08:03,175
it has to be paid into an
account in the child's name.

135
00:08:03,455 --> 00:08:05,455
Ready to open one? Here's how.

136
00:08:05,935 --> 00:08:08,175
Step one: choose a provider.

137
00:08:08,495 --> 00:08:11,135
That can be your bank,
or an investment platform.

138
00:08:11,695 --> 00:08:15,855
Step two: have your ID ready,
and possibly a proof of address,

139
00:08:15,895 --> 00:08:17,295
for the normal FICA checks.

140
00:08:17,855 --> 00:08:23,455
Step three: choose what goes inside,
cash or investments, and check the fees.

141
00:08:23,935 --> 00:08:27,855
Step four: set up a debit order
that stays under the limit,

142
00:08:27,935 --> 00:08:32,655
like R3,833 a month.
And watch the deadline.

143
00:08:32,975 --> 00:08:35,775
The tax year ends on the
last day of February,

144
00:08:35,855 --> 00:08:39,455
and some providers stop accepting
deposits a few days before that.

145
00:08:40,015 --> 00:08:44,815
Let's recap. A TFSA is a wrapper
that protects your money from tax.

146
00:08:45,215 --> 00:08:50,175
You can put in R46,000 a year,
and R500,000 for life.

147
00:08:50,255 --> 00:08:52,695
Growth doesn't count towards the limits.

148
00:08:52,895 --> 00:08:56,655
Don't go over the limit, don't
treat it like an emergency fund,

149
00:08:56,735 --> 00:09:00,495
and if you switch providers,
transfer instead of withdrawing.

150
00:09:00,735 --> 00:09:03,935
Start small if you have
to, but start early.

151
00:09:04,055 --> 00:09:06,095
Your future self will thank you.

152
00:09:06,175 --> 00:09:09,055
If this helped,
subscribe to Money From Zero.

153
00:09:09,175 --> 00:09:12,175
Every week, we make one
more piece of money simple.

154
00:09:12,255 --> 00:09:15,775
This video is general information,
not financial advice.

